<h2>The situation</h2>

<p>
  A portfolio of more than 110 client websites was running on legacy custom
  WordPress themes. The themes were increasingly expensive to maintain, slow,
  and difficult to extend. The business decision to move everything onto a
  modern block-based framework was straightforward. The SEO risk was not.
</p>

<p>
  These are multi-location healthcare practices. For most of them, organic
  search is the single largest source of new patient inquiries, and the
  conversion is a phone call or a booked appointment rather than an online
  purchase. A migration that quietly dropped a handful of ranking location
  pages would not show up as a traffic chart anomaly first &mdash; it would show
  up as a practice owner asking why the phone stopped ringing.
</p>

<h2>What made it genuinely hard</h2>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Scale with variation.</strong> 110+ sites sounds like one problem
    solved 110 times. It is not. Each site had accumulated its own URL quirks,
    orphaned pages, legacy campaign landing pages, and locally-added content
    that nobody had inventoried.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Templated structures hiding real differences.</strong> Sites that
    looked identical in the CMS had meaningfully different internal link graphs
    and indexation profiles once crawled.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Distributed stakeholders.</strong> Every site had a marketing
    manager and a practice owner who needed to understand what was changing and
    why, in terms that had nothing to do with crawl budget.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>The approach</h2>

<h3>1. Inventory before architecture</h3>
<p>
  Nothing moved until each site had a complete URL inventory reconciled across
  crawl data, Google Search Console performance data, and analytics. The
  reconciliation matters: a page with no internal links and no crawl depth can
  still be the top organic entry point for a specific market. Crawl-only
  inventories miss those; GSC-only inventories miss the pages that should be
  consolidated.
</p>

<h3>2. Information architecture driven by search demand</h3>
<p>
  The new framework was an opportunity to fix structures that had grown
  organically over years &mdash; service pages that competed with each other,
  location pages buried three levels deep, and thin content that diluted topical
  focus. I mapped the target IA against actual query demand per market rather
  than replicating the existing tree by default.
</p>

<h3>3. Redirect mapping as a managed artifact</h3>
<p>
  Redirects were treated as a reviewable deliverable, not a cleanup step.
  Pattern-level regex rules handled predictable structural changes at scale;
  one-to-one mappings handled the pages that carried real equity. Every map was
  validated before launch and re-validated after, with explicit checks for
  redirect chains, loops, and anything resolving to a soft 404.
</p>

<h3>4. Post-launch monitoring with a defined response</h3>
<p>
  Launch is the start of the measurement window, not the end of the project.
  Each migration had a monitoring period watching indexation coverage, query-level
  position changes, and conversion actions &mdash; with agreed thresholds for when
  a change warranted intervention versus when it was normal post-migration
  volatility.
</p>

<h2>What I'd tell another strategist</h2>

<p>
  The single highest-leverage habit in a migration of this size is
  <strong>refusing to treat the redirect map as a technical afterthought</strong>.
  It is the artifact that preserves years of accumulated equity, and it deserves
  the same review rigor as the design.
</p>

<p>
  The second is communication cadence. A migration that goes perfectly but
  surprises the stakeholder still damages trust. Telling a marketing manager
  in advance that they may see two to four weeks of ranking fluctuation turns a
  support escalation into an expected event.
</p>
